# Copy to .env (gitignored). Everything has a working default.

# HF_TOKEN=hf_...          # the model is PUBLIC - only needed behind a proxy

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE MEMORY KNOB, and the one number the rest are derived from
#
# 0.78 of 121 GiB unified is ~94 GiB claimed at startup. The token pool, the KV
# cache size and max_running_requests are all OUTCOMES of it, computed at boot -
# they are not settings. ./report.sh prints what you actually got.
#
# The reference DGX Spark kit at 0.78 got: ~4.93M pool tokens, ~14.1 GiB of
# FP8 KV, 48 concurrent requests, ~1,048,570 max input.
#
# This is a fraction of THIS GPU's memory, so it does not transfer. On a 32 GiB
# 5090 the same 0.78 OOMs at startup or leaves a near-empty cache - the README's
# memory table has the starting points for other cards.
# MEM_FRACTION_STATIC=0.78

# Decode-graph scratch AND a decode concurrency cap. 4 here, 16 on an H100.
# Raising it buys batch throughput out of the same pool the KV cache lives in.
# CUDA_GRAPH_MAX_BS_DECODE=4

# Unset by default and that is deliberate: the checkpoint's config.json already
# says 1,048,576, and the reference run does not pass this flag. Setting it
# lower does NOT free KV on a hybrid model - the floor is mamba state plus the
# draft cache, and neither moves with the cap.
# CONTEXT_LENGTH=1048576

# Derived from the cache budget at startup. Set it only to cap concurrency
# below what the pool would allow.
# MAX_RUNNING_REQUESTS=48

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SPECULATIVE DECODING - three published speculators, not interchangeable
#
# Measured on a DGX Spark (code generation, single stream / 8 concurrent):
#
#   none     81.3 / 241.7 tok/s     the THROUGHPUT-optimal choice
#   dflash   95.5 / 268.6 tok/s     +17% / +11%
#   mtp     111.4 / 302.3 tok/s     +37% / +25%
#   dspark  124.2 / 354.6 tok/s     +53% / +47%   <- default, wins on both axes
#
# `none` also hands back ~28 GiB: the DSpark draft keeps its own bf16 KV cache,
# which is the largest single allocation in this server and bigger than the
# weights. If the pool is your binding constraint, turn the drafter OFF before
# you lower MEM_FRACTION_STATIC.
# SPEC_METHOD=dspark

# BLOCK SIZE IS GAMMA, NOT THE VERIFY WINDOW. SGLang's own help: the verify
# window is gamma + 1, so 3 drafts three tokens and verifies four. Comment it
# out entirely and SGLang infers gamma from the draft checkpoint's own
# block_size - the safer choice if a future draft revision changes it.
# DSPARK_BLOCK_SIZE=3
# DFLASH_BLOCK_SIZE=4

# MTP drives the checkpoint's OWN prediction heads through SGLang's EAGLE path,
# so the "draft model path" is the target model and there is no second
# download. That looks like a typo and is what the cookbook specifies.
# MTP_STEPS=5
# MTP_TOPK=1
# MTP_DRAFT_TOKENS=6

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Weights. Public, OpenMDW-1.1.
# MODEL=nvidia/NVIDIA-Nemotron-3.5-Lightning-30B-A3B-NVFP4
# DSPARK_DRAFT=nvidia/NVIDIA-Nemotron-3.5-Lightning-30B-A3B-NVFP4-DSpark
# DFLASH_DRAFT=nvidia/NVIDIA-Nemotron-3.5-Lightning-30B-A3B-NVFP4-DFlash
# SERVED_NAME=nemotron-3.5-lightning

# A `dev-` tag tracks upstream and can be rebuilt in place. Pin the digest if
# you want the guarantee in writing.
# IMAGE=lmsysorg/sglang:dev-nemotron3-5-lightning

# float16, not bfloat16 and not auto - the dtype the published 716 MiB mamba
# cache figure was measured at.
# MAMBA_SSM_DTYPE=float16

# `auto` resolves to FP8 e4m3fn for this checkpoint. Forcing bf16 does not make
# it more accurate, it makes the pool roughly four times smaller.
# KV_CACHE_DTYPE=auto

# 8894, so it does not collide with the other serving workspaces.
# PORT=8894

# 0.0.0.0, and the container runs on --network host, so the endpoint is on the
# LAN. That is what lets the peer node and a laptop use it; ufw is the boundary.
# Set 127.0.0.1 to keep it on the box.
# HOST_BIND=0.0.0.0

# How long `ws up` waits for /health, in 2-second steps. 450 = 15 minutes, which
# matches the vLLM workspaces' healthcheck start_period for the same reason:
# weights off NVMe plus CUDA-graph capture is minutes, not seconds.
# READY_TIMEOUT_STEPS=450
